# Hardware and platform reference collection

This directory holds vendor documents, board design files, firmware packages,
and locally checked out upstream software related to the thesis targets. It is
a **reference collection** for Stage 0 investigation. It is not an implementation
repository, a source of approved architecture, or evidence that HAVEN boots or
isolates a device on a board.

## Start here

- [Radxa Orion O6 and O6N](radxa-orion-o6/README.md): board-specific design
  references and the shared Linux source checkout.
- [Orange Pi 6 and 6 Plus](orange-pi-6/README.md): board manuals, schematics,
  BIOS files, and the vendor build bundle.
- [Shared CIX/SKY1 references](common/README.md): firmware, kernel, graphics,
  and platform documentation that can apply across multiple boards.

## Directory map

| Path                                            | Contents and role                                                                                                                            |
| ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `radxa-orion-o6/base/`                          | Orion O6 board-level schematic, component maps, mechanical files, and BIOS archive.                                                          |
| `radxa-orion-o6/n/`                             | Orion O6N schematic, component map, mechanical files, and BIOS archive. Treat O6N as its own board variant.                                  |
| `radxa-orion-o6/common/linux/`                  | A Git checkout of Radxa's Linux source/build tree; consult its own README and record its revision before using it.                           |
| `orange-pi-6/base/`                             | Orange Pi 6 schematic, BIOS and Linux manuals, Windows installation manual, and BIOS/driver bundle.                                          |
| `orange-pi-6/plus/`                             | Orange Pi 6 Plus schematic, BIOS/Linux/Android/OpenHarmony/Windows manuals, and supporting model package. `Back up/` contains older manuals. |
| `orange-pi-6/common/`                           | Shared Orange Pi 6 family assets, toolchain archive, media, and the `orangepi-build` Git checkout.                                           |
| `common/cix-bios/`                              | CIX P1 UEFI source checkout and its upstream README/submodules.                                                                              |
| `common/cix-gpu-kmd/`                           | CIX GPU kernel module source checkout and upstream README.                                                                                   |
| `common/cix-linux-main.wiki/`                   | CIX mainline Linux notes, including an Orion O6 setup guide and video acceleration notes.                                                    |
| `common/cix-manifest.wiki/`                     | CIX Linux build and image workflow notes; includes English and Chinese pages.                                                                |
| `common/linux-mainline.wiki/`                   | General Linux mainline wiki material. Check page scope before applying it to a specific board.                                               |
| `common/sky1-linux-build/`                      | Sky1 kernel packaging scripts and upstream README.                                                                                           |
| `common/CIX-Multimedia-Open-Source-Support.pdf` | CIX multimedia support reference document.                                                                                                   |

## Evidence and provenance rules

Keep claims tied to a source and state their status:

1. **Reference fact:** what a specific manual or schematic states. Record file,
   document version, page or sheet, and the exact board variant it covers.
2. **Source observation:** what a checked-out source tree or config contains.
   Record its Git remote, commit, and relevant path/symbol. A driver or config
   option does not establish that the hardware works.
3. **Hardware observation:** what was measured on a named physical board. Record
   board revision, firmware image/version/hash, boot configuration, toolchain,
   commands, logs, and any attached device.
4. **HAVEN result:** what the pinned HAVEN build did under a reproducible test.
   Record the isolation property tested, positive and negative cases, and raw
   evidence. QEMU and physical-board results are separate evidence.

Prefer revisioned board schematics and current manuals for board-specific
descriptions. Treat firmware archives as candidates until identified and tested
on a board. Treat community wiki reports and vendor build trees as leads. Older
files under `Back up/` are historical references. If references disagree,
preserve the disagreement and resolve it with exact hardware evidence.

For every claimed HAVEN target, establish the board revision, firmware and boot
path, EL2 entry, ACPI or Device Tree handoff, memory map, GIC and timer setup,
PCIe requester identifiers, and SMMU/IOMMU mappings. Do not infer an isolatable
DMA path from an SMMU-enabled kernel configuration.

## Git checkouts and upstream material

Some subdirectories are independent Git checkouts and may contain nested
submodules. Their revisions can change independently of this workspace. Before
using one as evidence, run `git -C <path> remote -v`, `git -C <path> rev-parse
HEAD`, and `git -C <path> status --short`; record the outputs in the experiment
or audit notes. Read the checkout's own README when present. This collection
does not claim a single synchronized upstream snapshot.

Do not edit upstream README files to add workspace commentary. Put collection
context in these index files, and keep any necessary local patches explicit and
reviewable. Check licenses before copying code or redistributing files.

## Stage 0 use

Create one board record for each of O6, O6N, Orange Pi 6, and Orange Pi 6 Plus.
For each, link the exact local references, record known facts and unresolved
questions, then classify readiness as `unknown`, `blocked`, `probe-ready`, or
`verified`. Status can differ by evidence type: these four boards are currently
`blocked` for physical validation because no SBCs or boot logs are available;
source and document audits can continue. Files in this directory alone do not
qualify a board as supported.

The current workspace audit, host record, source revisions, and initial
four-board matrix are in [`../STAGE0_BASELINE.md`](../STAGE0_BASELINE.md).
